From 1e2db4b69e1c8bb2bd54d97e4e76e2f39ec022fb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Mohammed <91819504+mohammed7s@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sun, 8 Nov 2026 14:04:15 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] computercontrol: harden revocation, user lifecycle and
 session creation

Close the gaps between a grant being withdrawn and a session existing:

 - Settings and the consent dialog app can never be automation targets.
   They are rejected before any other check, even if DeviceConfig lists
   them, so automation cannot be used to edit its own permissions.
 - Withdrawing a target's user switch also clears every agent's saved
   consent for that target in that user. Re-enabling it asks again.
 - Package removal is handled for every user, and removing a user drops
   its records from both the user allowlist and the consent store.
   Closing sessions on revocation is scoped to the affected user.
 - Version 4 session requests are rejected, so the version 5
   foreground-window requirement always applies.
 - Session creation is asynchronous. Owner, target and consent are
   checked again immediately before a constructed session is published.
   "Allow once" is preserved: only targets approved by the current
   dialog skip the saved-consent lookup.

Creation reserves capacity under the session registry lock and constructs
the session outside it. An authorization generation, bumped by every
grant edit, rejects creations that overlap an edit; a rejected session is
closed outside the lock. Nothing that queries other services or calls
clients runs while the lock is held. This avoids a lock-order cycle with
WindowManager, whose Back handling holds the global lock while querying
isOnComputerControlDisplay().

Test: atest VirtualDeviceServicesTests:ComputerControlSessionProcessorTest
Test: atest VirtualDeviceServicesTests:ComputerControlAllowlistControllerTest
Test: atest VirtualDeviceServicesTests:ComputerControlUserAllowlistTest

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
